Relevant columns & their potential usage  
• “gene_id” (DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE) – the gene we will group‑by in every study.  
• “study_id” (DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE) – the GWAS / molecular‑trait study in which the association was observed.  
• “pval” (DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE) – raw two‑sided P‑value that will be minimised to find the strongest variant per (gene, study) pair.  
• “tag_chrom”, “tag_pos”, “tag_ref”, “tag_alt” (DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE) – genomic co‑ordinates of the associated variant, returned with the result set.  
• “lead_chrom”, “lead_pos”, “lead_ref”, “lead_alt” (DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE) – alternative variant coordinates in case the tag_* fields are NULL.  
• “pval_mantissa”, “pval_exponent” (DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE) – occasionally used instead of “pval”; can be combined to reconstruct an exact P‑value.  
• “gene_name” (GENES) – human‑readable gene symbol to show in the final list.  
• “study_id”, “trait_reported” (STUDIES) – optional, lets us display the phenotype but not required for the core ranking.  
Main conditions that will be handy  
• WHERE "pval" IS NOT NULL                         -- ignore missing P‑values  
• QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY "gene_id","study_id" ORDER BY "pval") = 1  
• ORDER BY "pval" ASC                              -- rank strongest associations  
• LIMIT 10                                         -- return top 10 rows  

Below are ten incremental Snowflake SQL snippets (each limited to 20 rows unless stated differently) that progressively explore the data and culminate in one query that fulfils the task.  
All identifiers are double‑quoted and fully qualified.

```sql
-- 1. Quick peek at the association table
SELECT 
  "gene_id",
  "study_id",
  "pval",
  "tag_chrom",
  "tag_pos"
FROM "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. How many genes and records have a non‑null p‑value?
SELECT 
  COUNT(*)                          AS "n_rows",
  COUNT(DISTINCT "gene_id")         AS "n_genes"
FROM "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE"
WHERE "pval" IS NOT NULL;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect the GENES table to get gene symbols
SELECT 
  "gene_id",
  "gene_name",
  "chr",
  "start",
  "end"
FROM "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."GENES"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Join a handful of associations to gene symbols for readability
SELECT 
  d."gene_id",
  g."gene_name",
  d."study_id",
  d."pval"
FROM "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE"  d
JOIN "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."GENES"                 g
  ON d."gene_id" = g."gene_id"
WHERE d."pval" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. For ONE study, keep the lowest‑p‑value variant per gene
SELECT *
FROM (
  SELECT
    d."gene_id",
    d."tag_chrom",
    d."tag_pos",
    d."tag_ref",
    d."tag_alt",
    d."pval",
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY d."gene_id" ORDER BY d."pval") AS rn
  FROM "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE" d
  WHERE d."study_id" = 'GCST90002340'
    AND d."pval" IS NOT NULL
) sub
WHERE rn = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Same logic but across ALL studies: keep 1 variant per (gene, study)
SELECT *
FROM (
  SELECT
    "gene_id",
    "study_id",
    "tag_chrom",
    "tag_pos",
    "tag_ref",
    "tag_alt",
    "pval",
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER(
      PARTITION BY "gene_id","study_id"
      ORDER BY "pval"
    ) AS rn
  FROM "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE"
  WHERE "pval" IS NOT NULL
) AS ranked
WHERE rn = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Check distribution of the best p‑values (log10 scale)
SELECT 
  ROUND(LOG10("pval"),2) AS "log10_pval_bin",
  COUNT(*)               AS "n_associations"
FROM (
  SELECT
    "gene_id",
    "study_id",
    "pval",
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY "gene_id","study_id" ORDER BY "pval") AS rn
  FROM "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE"
  WHERE "pval" IS NOT NULL
) sub
WHERE rn = 1
GROUP BY "log10_pval_bin"
ORDER BY "log10_pval_bin";
```

```sql
-- 8. Rank all (gene, study) representatives by p‑value
SELECT
  "gene_id",
  "study_id",
  "pval"
FROM (
  SELECT
    "gene_id",
    "study_id",
    "pval",
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY "gene_id","study_id" ORDER BY "pval") AS rn
  FROM "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE"
  WHERE "pval" IS NOT NULL
) sub
WHERE rn = 1
ORDER BY "pval" ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Same as #8 but joined to gene symbols
SELECT
  d."gene_id",
  g."gene_name",
  d."study_id",
  d."pval"
FROM (
  SELECT
    "gene_id",
    "study_id",
    "pval",
    "tag_chrom",
    "tag_pos",
    "tag_ref",
    "tag_alt",
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY "gene_id","study_id" ORDER BY "pval") AS rn
  FROM "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE"
  WHERE "pval" IS NOT NULL
) d
JOIN "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."GENES" g
  ON d."gene_id" = g."gene_id"
WHERE d.rn = 1
ORDER BY d."pval" ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL: top 10 genes with the strongest associations across ALL studies
SELECT
  d."gene_id",
  g."gene_name",
  d."study_id",
  d."pval",
  COALESCE(d."tag_chrom", d."lead_chrom") AS "chrom",
  COALESCE(d."tag_pos" , d."lead_pos"  ) AS "pos",
  COALESCE(d."tag_ref" , d."lead_ref"  ) AS "ref",
  COALESCE(d."tag_alt" , d."lead_alt"  ) AS "alt"
FROM (
  /* one record per (gene,study) – the variant with the smallest P‑value */
  SELECT
    "gene_id",
    "study_id",
    "pval",
    "tag_chrom", "tag_pos", "tag_ref", "tag_alt",
    "lead_chrom","lead_pos","lead_ref","lead_alt",
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER(
      PARTITION BY "gene_id","study_id"
      ORDER BY "pval"
    ) AS rn
  FROM "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."DISEASE_VARIANT_GENE"
  WHERE "pval" IS NOT NULL
) d
JOIN "OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS_2"."OPEN_TARGETS_GENETICS"."GENES" g
  ON d."gene_id" = g."gene_id"
WHERE d.rn = 1
ORDER BY d."pval" ASC
LIMIT 10;
```

These ten queries gradually build an understanding of the tables, verify assumptions, and culminate in the final SQL that extracts the ten genes whose best variant in any study exhibits the most significant (smallest) P‑value.
